#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3. First principles"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "The patterns rest on a handful of ideas. Each one is stated below as a claim, together with the source it comes from."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.1 Event time is not processing time, and completeness is a guess"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Akidau et al. separate *event time* from *processing time* and argue that a system processing unbounded, out-of-order data \"will never know if or when we have seen all of our data, only that new data will arrive, old data may be retracted\" [1]. The Dataflow Model makes the trade-off explicit with four questions: *what* is computed, *where* in event time (windows), *when* in processing time results are emitted (triggers, driven by watermarks), and *how* refinements relate (discarding, accumulating, or accumulating-and-retracting panes) [1]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "This has three consequences for backfills. First, partition by event time, not load time; otherwise re-running \"yesterday\" doesn't mean anything stable. Second, a *watermark* is only a heuristic statement that the input for a time range is probably complete, and backfills exist largely because that heuristic is sometimes wrong. Third, when a backfill changes a result that was already emitted, downstream needs either the whole result replaced (discarding, or overwriting) or an explicit retraction. Quietly changing the result in place is neither."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.2 The log is the source of truth; tables are projections"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Kreps: a log of all changes \"from the beginning\" is a complete representation of a dataset; tables and indexes are projections of it, and can be rebuilt by replaying it [2]. In \"Questioning the Lambda Architecture\" he proposes *Kappa* reprocessing: when logic changes, start a second instance of the job from the beginning of the retained log, write to a **new** output table, switch readers when it catches up, then delete the old job and table [3]. This removes the Lambda requirement to maintain the same logic twice (batch and speed layers) [3]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Helland makes the general case: immutable data is what lets distributed systems coordinate, and updatable stores are layered on immutable files (LSM trees, copy-on-write) [5]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "So keep raw inputs immutable, and keep them long enough to replay. If the raw input is gone, you can't do a logic-change backfill (S2) at all. You'd be restating from a derived table instead, and that carries its errors forward."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.3 Functional data engineering: pure tasks over immutable partitions"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Beauchemin (2018) applies functional programming to batch pipelines [6]. In his approach, tasks are *pure*, meaning the same inputs always give the same output and the only side effect is writing their own target. They're also *idempotent*, and they write *immutable partitions*, which are replaced whole and never changed in place. Raw data goes into a *persistent staging area* that's kept indefinitely, so any partition can be recomputed. Slowly changing dimensions are handled by taking a snapshot of each dimension for each partition, rather than changing rows. With these rules in place, a backfill is simply the same task run for older partitions."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.4 Idempotency and exactly-once *effects*"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "8"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "In practice, delivery across a network is at-least-once, so you get \"exactly once\" as an *effect*, by making the write idempotent [7]{1}. Helland defines an idempotent operation as one where repeating it has the same effect as doing it once, and argues that any system that retries messages needs it [7]. Here are practical ways to get it:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Use deterministic keys.** Derive the row key from the content or the domain, as a natural key or a hash of stable fields, and never from the load time or a sequence assigned during the load. Then a retried load produces the same keys, and a MERGE or an overwrite turns it into a no-op."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Replace whole slices.** Replace the whole slice in one atomic step (section 4.1)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Use commit tokens.** Record the job id together with the slice id or version, inside the same atomic commit as the data, and skip any commit whose token is already there. Delta Lake's `txnAppId` and `txnVersion` options do exactly this: a write whose version is at or below the last one recorded for that app id is ignored [16]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.5 Determinism of transformations"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "You can only compare a backfill with the original run if the transformation is deterministic, so that the same inputs and the same logic version give byte-identical outputs, or at least row-identical ones. That means removing every source of non-determinism: reads of the wall clock (`now()`), unseeded randomness, iteration over unordered containers, `LIMIT` without `ORDER BY`, the order of floating-point aggregation, \"latest\" lookups resolved when the job runs rather than as of the slice's event time, and any dependence on the run date instead of the slice date. Determinism is what makes parallel-run diffs (section 4.8) meaningful, because any difference is then caused by the logic or the input, not by noise. (This follows from first principles; there's no single source.)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.6 Bitemporal modelling"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Snodgrass distinguishes *valid time* (when a fact holds in reality) from *transaction time* (when the database knew it) and shows how to model, query and correct both in SQL [10]. SQL:2011 standardised application-time period tables (valid time), system-versioned tables (transaction time) and bitemporal tables combining both [11]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Backfills need this because a backfill that corrects history is a *transaction time* event about *valid time* facts. Without both axes, you can't answer \"what did we report for March, as of the April board meeting?\", and a restatement wipes out the evidence of what was published before. Lakehouse table formats give you transaction time for free for a whole table, through time travel to a snapshot, but not for each row. When consumers need to see corrections explicitly, you need per-row `recorded_at` and `superseded_at` columns."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.7 Slowly changing dimensions"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Kimball describes several types of slowly changing dimension [12]. Type 1 overwrites the attribute, so history is lost. Type 2 adds a row with effective dates, so history is kept. Type 3 adds a \"previous value\" column, and types 0, 4, 5, 6 and 7 are variants and hybrids (DT #152)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "For backfills, the rule is that **a type 2 dimension must be backfilled in event-time order**, because each new version closes the one before it. If you load out of order, you either get overlapping validity ranges or have to rebuild the key's whole history. A safe alternative is to rebuild the full type 2 history for the affected keys from the immutable change log in a single pass. Beauchemin's \"dimension snapshots\" [6] give the same result without any constraint on order."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.8 Recompute versus incremental maintenance"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "The literature on view maintenance sets out the choice. You can recompute a derived table in full, or maintain it incrementally from the changes to its inputs [13]. Incremental maintenance costs less for each change, but it's only correct for certain kinds of view. Aggregates, for example, can maintain themselves under inserts, but not always under deletes or updates. Incremental maintenance also carries forward any past error. A full recompute is always correct with respect to its inputs and logic, but its cost grows with the size of the history."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "22"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "So normal operation can be incremental, but **a logic change (S2) needs a recompute** of every slice the change affects, because incremental maintenance only applies the new logic to new changes. Many orchestration tools give you this choice directly, for example dbt's `--full-refresh`, or microbatch with an explicit range [21]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4. Pattern catalogue: analytical platform"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Each pattern is described under the same headings: **Problem**, **Forces**, **Solution**, **Consequences**, **Verification**, **Sources**, and **Maps to**, which covers (a) an open-source stack and (b) Databricks."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.1 Slice replacement (partition overwrite)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** When you re-run a time slice of a derived table (S2, S3), it mustn't double-count or leave stale rows behind."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Appending is cheap, but it isn't idempotent. Deleting rows and then inserting is two operations, so it can fail halfway. And readers must never see the slice empty, or see it twice."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Treat the slice, a partition or a predicate range, as the unit of work. Compute the whole slice, then replace exactly that slice in a single atomic commit. You can do that with `INSERT OVERWRITE` and dynamic partition overwrite, or with an overwrite that has an explicit predicate (`replaceWhere`). The predicate must be the same one the computation used to select its inputs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Re-runs are idempotent, and slices can run in any order. The slice has to be computable from its inputs alone (a pure task, 3.3), so this pattern doesn't suit tables without a natural slice. Watch out for one danger. In Spark's default **static** overwrite mode, an `INSERT OVERWRITE` without a `PARTITION` clause replaces **all** partitions, and Iceberg's documentation shows it deleting every row in the table while writing a single day [17]. So always use dynamic mode or an explicit predicate. Iceberg recommends `MERGE INTO` over dynamic overwrite, because the scope that gets overwritten can change if the partitioning evolves [17]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Compare the slice's row count and checksum before and after. Check that rows outside the predicate haven't changed, by comparing snapshot diffs: the commit should only touch files belonging to that slice. Then run the backfill twice and check that the outputs are identical."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "16"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [6]{1}[17]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Spark + Iceberg with `spark.sql.sources.partitionOverwriteMode=dynamic`, or Delta `replaceWhere`; dbt `insert_overwrite` strategy. (b) Delta `replaceWhere` / `INSERT INTO ... REPLACE WHERE`, dynamic partition overwrite; Delta `txnAppId`/`txnVersion` for commit-level idempotency."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.2 Keyed upsert with recency guard"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You're loading or correcting entities that are identified by a key, such as learners, institutions or sheets, and the backfill may carry older versions than the ones already there."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** MERGE is only idempotent if the keys are deterministic. A backfill running alongside live ingestion can overwrite a fresher row with a stale one. And if the source has duplicate keys, MERGE either fails or behaves non-deterministically."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** `MERGE` on the **natural key**, and add a **recency guard** to the update clause, so a row is only updated if the incoming version is newer according to a monotonic version field. That field might be the source LSN, the source's commit sequence or a record version. For example: `WHEN MATCHED AND s.src_version > t.src_version THEN UPDATE`. Remove duplicates from the source first, keeping the highest version of each key, so that at most one source row matches each target row. For deletes, carry tombstones with a version, rather than inferring a deletion from a missing row."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** It's safe to run alongside live ingestion, and safe to repeat. It does need a version field you can trust; a wall-clock `updated_at` is a weak substitute (see 4.3). MERGE rewrites every file that contains a matched row, so a large backfill through MERGE can be slower than slice replacement."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** For a sample of keys, check that the target holds the highest-version row across all sources. Re-run the backfill and check that no rows changed. The number of keys in the target should equal the number of distinct keys in the sources, minus those with tombstones."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "17"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [7]{1}. Iceberg notes that MERGE needs at most one source row for each target row [17]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Iceberg/Delta `MERGE INTO`; dbt incremental `merge` with `unique_key`; Postgres `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE ... WHERE excluded.v > t.v`. (b) Delta `MERGE INTO`; Lakeflow declarative pipelines' `AUTO CDC` / `APPLY CHANGES` with a sequencing column does the same guarded upsert **[mapping not re-checked against current docs]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.3 High-water-mark incremental extract (with overlap)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You want to pull only the new or changed rows from a source table."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** This is cheap and simple, and it only needs an `updated_at` column or an id that always increases. But the order in which rows are committed isn't the same as the order of their timestamps."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Record the high-water mark (HWM) after each successful load. The next run reads `updated_at >= HWM - overlap`, removes duplicates by key and version, and upserts the result (4.2). Only advance the HWM after the write has committed, and then only to `min(max_seen, now - safety_lag)`. If the source has a cursor in commit order, such as a log sequence number, a Postgres cursor based on `xmin`, or a sequence that an outbox assigns at commit, use that instead."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Without the overlap, you lose rows. In Postgres, `now()` returns the *start time of the transaction* [18]. So a long transaction that starts at 10:00 and commits at 10:05 writes `updated_at = 10:00`. If the extractor ran at 10:02 and moved its HWM to 10:02, it never sees that row. Clock skew between application servers has the same effect [9]. Extraction by HWM can't see hard deletes at all. And the overlap creates duplicates, so whatever receives the data must be idempotent (4.2)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Regularly reconcile every key between the source and the sink (4.8), comparing counts and key sets for each day. Alert if the HWM stops moving, or moves backwards."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "18"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [9]{1}, and first principles."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) An Airflow or Dagster task with a stored cursor, or dbt incremental models that filter with `is_incremental()` on `>=` and a lookback. (b) The same in Lakeflow Jobs, although for databases you should prefer Lakeflow Connect CDC where it's available."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.4 Snapshot and log stitching (onboarding a live source)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You're bringing in a live OLTP source with years of history (S4). You need a full copy plus every change made after it, with no gap and no lost update where the two meet, and you can't lock the source."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** A consistent full snapshot of a big table takes hours, and locking the tables for that long isn't acceptable. If you start CDC after the snapshot, you leave a gap. If you start it before, you get overlaps that have to be resolved key by key. And the snapshot has to be able to resume after an interruption."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "19"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Use DBLog, from Netflix [14]. Keep the change-log stream running the whole time, and take the snapshot in **chunks**, ordered by primary key. For each chunk, write a *low watermark* event into the log (through a small watermark table in the source), select the chunk, and then write a *high watermark*. When the log reader reaches the high watermark, it drops any row from the chunk whose key appeared in log events between the low and high watermarks, because the log event is newer. Then it emits the rest of the chunk and carries on. There are no locks, the log never stalls, and you can pause and resume chunks. Debezium's incremental snapshots implement the same algorithm. You trigger one by inserting an `execute-snapshot` signal, optionally with an `additional-conditions` filter, into a signalling table. The chunk size defaults to 1,024 rows, and progress is stored in the connector's offsets, so the snapshot resumes after a crash [15]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** The tables need primary keys. Consumers see a mix of `read` and `update` events for the same key, in an order that isn't obvious, so the downstream step must be an idempotent keyed upsert (4.2) [15]. You also add a watermark or signal table to the source schema. On the other hand, you can re-snapshot just part of the data, such as one table or one key range, to repair a gap. That makes this a gap-fill tool (S3) as well as an onboarding tool."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Once the snapshot is complete, reconcile the row count and a hash of the keys for each table, or each key range, between the source and the sink (4.8). Read the source from a replica at a known LSN. Then check that the replication slot's lag has returned to its usual level."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "15"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [14]{1}[19]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Debezium on Kafka and Kafka Connect, or Debezium Server, feeding Iceberg or Delta through MERGE, with Postgres logical replication slots. (b) Lakeflow Connect for the databases it supports, since it manages the initial snapshot and CDC. Otherwise, Debezium into a landing zone, followed by a CDC upsert in a declarative pipeline **[Lakeflow Connect internals not verified]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.5 Transactional outbox"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** An operational service has to publish its changes to the platform reliably, and, when it's being onboarded, its history too."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Writing to the database and writing to a broker are two separate operations that aren't atomic together (a \"dual write\"), so either can fail after the other has succeeded. Distributed transactions are rarely available, and rarely a good idea."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Write the business change, and an event row in an `outbox` table, **in the same database transaction**. A relay, either a poller or log-based CDC on the outbox table, publishes the outbox rows in commit order [20]. For history, the service or a one-off job writes synthetic \"snapshot\" events for existing entities into the same outbox, keyed and versioned just like live events. That way, history and live changes travel the same path."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Delivery is at-least-once: after a crash the relay can publish a row twice, so consumers must be idempotent [20]. Order is preserved within each aggregate. The outbox needs pruning, and developers can forget to write the event."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Have consumers record the last event id they processed for each aggregate, and reconcile the number of outbox rows with the number of events consumed each day."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "14"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [20]{1}. DBLog's motivation section rejects dual writes for the same reason."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) A Postgres outbox table with Debezium's outbox event router. (b) A Lakebase table as the outbox, with CDC or sync to Delta **[Lakebase-to-Delta sync mechanics not re-verified here]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.6 Shadow recompute and swap (blue/green tables)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need to recompute a whole table with new logic (S2), without consumers seeing a half-updated mix of old and new results, and with a way back."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Rewriting in place exposes partial states and destroys the old answer. A recompute takes time, and live data keeps arriving while it runs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** This is Kreps's Kappa reprocessing, generalised [3]. Run the new logic from the start of the retained input into a **new** table (or a branch, 4.7), and let it catch up with the live input. Compare it with the old output (4.8), and then switch consumers over with an atomic pointer change, such as redefining a view, changing a catalog alias, fast-forwarding a branch or renaming a table. Keep the old table for a defined fallback window, then drop it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Storage and compute double while the two overlap. Readers see either all the old results or all the new ones, never a mix. The raw inputs must have been kept (3.2), and consumers that cached results need a restatement notice (4.11)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Run a parallel-run diff (4.8). Every difference between the old and new outputs must be explained by the logic change. A diff of zero on the slices the change shouldn't affect makes a strong regression test."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "2"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [3]{1}[4]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) A new Iceberg table or branch, swapped in through a view or a catalog rename, or dbt with a versioned model (`v2`) and a view pointing at the active version. (b) A Delta table for each version, with consumers reading through a Unity Catalog view, swapped with `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.7 Write-audit-publish (WAP) on a branch"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** A backfill writes a lot of data, and any defect becomes visible to consumers the moment it commits."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Quality checks need the data to be written somewhere first. Copying data to a staging area and back doubles the I/O. And publication should be atomic."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** **Write** to an isolated version of the table, such as a branch, a staging table or a hidden snapshot. **Audit** it with data-quality checks and reconciliation. Then **publish** it with an atomic metadata operation. Netflix popularised the pattern (DataWorks Summit 2017, \"Whoops, the numbers are wrong! Scaling data quality @ Netflix\") **[unverified]**. In Iceberg, you set `write.wap.enabled=true`, create an audit branch, set `spark.wap.branch`, write and validate, and then run `CALL system.fast_forward('db.t', 'main', 'audit-branch')` [21a]. Nessie extends branches to commits across **several tables**, so a backfill that touches a dimension and its fact tables publishes atomically across all of them [23]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Publishing is a pointer move, so rolling back is too. Branches have a retention period, and the audit branch mustn't expire before you publish. A branch of a single table doesn't give you atomicity across tables, so consumers may see the new dimension with the old facts, unless you publish them together or use a catalog that handles several tables."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** The audit step is the verification. It covers schema checks, null and uniqueness constraints, reconciliation totals, and drift in distributions compared with the previous version. Declarative constraints in the style of Deequ, run as aggregation queries, are one systematic way to do it [24]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "23"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [21a]{1}[24], and the Netflix talk **[unverified]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Iceberg branches with `fast_forward`, and Nessie for several tables. lakeFS is an alternative if you want branching at the file level. (b) Delta doesn't have table branches, so use a **shallow clone** as the write target, audit it, and then publish with `replaceWhere`, MERGE or a view swap. Bear in mind that a shallow clone refers to the source's files, so it breaks if the source is `VACUUM`ed [25]. Delta time travel lets you roll back after publishing."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.8 Reconciliation and parallel-run diff"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need to prove that a backfill is complete and correct."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Comparing every row across systems is expensive. Legacy and new systems encode things differently, such as types, time zones and codes. And a job that succeeded tells you nothing about the data."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Reconcile in three tiers, starting with the cheapest:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Counts** for each slice (by date, school or source file), in both the source and the target."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Control totals** for each slice: sums of additive measures, the minimum and maximum of keys and timestamps, and the number of distinct keys."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Hashes**: hash each row's canonicalised business columns, then combine them in a way that doesn't depend on order, such as a sum or XOR of the row hashes, or a Merkle tree over key ranges. Do this for each slice, and only dig into the slices that don't match."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "27"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "For logic changes, run the old and new logic in parallel on the same inputs, and **diff** the outputs key by key. Then classify each difference as either expected, because the change caused it, or a defect. GitHub's Scientist library applies the same idea to code paths in production, and Stripe used it while migrating hundreds of millions of subscription records [26]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** You have to agree how values are canonicalised (trimming, case, time zone and decimal scale), or every row will mismatch. The hash tiers miss nothing in the columns they cover, but they tell you nothing about columns left out of the hash. Reconciliation has to read the source at a known, consistent point, such as a replica at an LSN or a frozen extract. Otherwise it races against live writes."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** The reconciliation report is an artefact in its own right. Store it with the backfill's run id, its logic version and the source position it was computed against."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "26"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [24]{1}[27], and Fowler and Thoughtworks on parallel running [28]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) SQL queries or a diffing tool over both sides, and Great Expectations, Deequ or dbt tests for constraints. (b) The same through Databricks SQL. Lakehouse Federation lets a single query read the legacy database and Delta side by side **[federation coverage of a given legacy DB not verified]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.9 Throttled, chunked extraction (protect the source)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need to read years of history from a production database, or from an API with rate limits, without hurting live users."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Big scans push useful data out of the cache and saturate I/O. On MVCC databases, a long-running read transaction also holds back vacuum and can bloat tables. APIs enforce rate limits. And an eight-hour extract that fails and starts again from zero wastes a lot of time."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Read from a **replica**, a **restored snapshot or backup**, or a **branch** (5.2), rather than the primary. Split the work into **chunks** by primary-key range (not `OFFSET`), each in a short transaction, and save the last completed chunk so the job can resume. **Throttle** on feedback signals, such as replication lag, the source's CPU and latency, and an API's `429` or `Retry-After` responses, and back off exponentially. Make the throttle settings adjustable while the job runs. gh-ost is the reference design here: you can pause it, it throttles on replica lag and on custom checks, and you can change the chunk size and maximum lag while it runs [29]. Stripe avoided production queries altogether by backfilling from offline Hadoop snapshots [27]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** It takes longer, and that's deliberate. Chunked reads aren't one consistent snapshot, so when you need consistency, combine them with log stitching (4.4) or read from a frozen copy."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Watch the source's latency and the replica lag during the run; the backfill shouldn't be visible on production dashboards. Reconcile chunk by chunk."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "29"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [27]{1}[14]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Airflow or Dagster with pools or concurrency limits, reading from a Postgres read replica. (b) Lakeflow Jobs with limited concurrency, reading from a Lakebase child branch or read replica rather than the production branch's primary compute."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.10 Dependency-aware orchestrated backfill"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** A backfill covers many slices and many tables that depend on each other. It has to run in the right order, with limited concurrency, and resume after a partial failure."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Downstream slices depend on upstream ones. Unlimited parallelism starves production schedules. Some slices are independent and can run in any order, but others, such as SCD2 and running totals, can't."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Let the orchestrator manage the backfill as an object in its own right:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Airflow 3.** You create backfills through the CLI, the API or the UI, and the scheduler runs them. A `reprocess_behavior` of `none`, `failed` or `completed` decides whether existing runs are run again. A `max_active_runs` setting on the backfill limits its concurrency separately from the DAG's own limit, `--run-backwards` processes the newest intervals first, and a dry run lists the intervals [30]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "32"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Dagster.** Partitioned assets take a `BackfillPolicy`. With `single_run()`, one run processes a range of partitions, which suits Spark and warehouse engines, although your code must then read `partition_key_range`. With `multi_run(max_partitions_per_run=n)`, each run is limited in size. Backfills across several assets follow the asset graph when their partitioning is compatible [31]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**dbt (1.9 or later).** The `microbatch` incremental strategy takes `event_time`, `batch_size`, `begin` and `lookback`. You target a backfill with `--event-time-start` and `--event-time-end`, and `dbt retry` re-runs only the batches that failed. Batches are independent and may run at the same time, and times are in UTC [22]. `--full-refresh` rebuilds an incremental model from scratch [21]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "As for order, load **reference data and dimensions before facts**. Within a table, go **oldest first** when a slice depends on the state left by the one before it. Go **newest first** when slices are independent and recent data matters most: users get useful results sooner, and if the backfill stops, the data that's missing is the oldest and least-used rather than the newest."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** The orchestrator's unit of work (one run per logical date) has to match the data's unit (a slice); otherwise the backfill isn't idempotent. You also have to budget the backfill's concurrency against production schedules."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** A dry run should list exactly the slices you intended. After the run, the orchestrator's state (every slice succeeded) should agree with the data's state (each slice reconciles)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "31"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [21]{1}[30]{2}[32]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Airflow 3 backfills, Dagster partitions or dbt microbatch. (b) Lakeflow Jobs **backfill runs**, which trigger one job run for each interval in a range, optionally in parallel, overriding the job's parameters. The job must use those parameters to select its slice [33]. For microbatch, use dbt tasks in Lakeflow Jobs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.11 Late data and restatement"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Records keep arriving for periods you've already reported on (S3), or a correction changes a number you've published."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Consumers want stable numbers, but completeness is only a heuristic (3.1). Regulators or boards may need to know what was reported, and when."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Reprocess a **lookback** window on every run, using dbt's `lookback` or overlapping Airflow intervals, so that data arriving late within the window is absorbed automatically [22]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Beyond the window, late data triggers an explicit **restatement**. That means a backfill of the affected slices through the normal path (4.1, 4.10), recorded as an event."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Keep **transaction time** on published outputs. At minimum that means table snapshots; where consumers need to see corrections, add row-level `recorded_at` or bitemporal columns [10]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Publish a restatement log that says which slices changed and why, their old and new totals, and the logic version."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Numbers change after they're published, but visibly and with a reason. The lookback costs compute on every run."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** For each restated slice, the restatement log shows the old and new control totals and links to the run id. Time travel to the snapshot before the restatement reproduces the old number."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "10"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [1]{1}[11]{2}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) dbt microbatch's `lookback`, and Iceberg tags to pin the snapshots as they were reported. (b) Delta time travel and table history, dbt microbatch, and a table for the restatement log."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.12 Logic version stamping"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** After a backfill, outputs produced by different code sit side by side, and nobody can tell which rows came from which logic."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Partial backfills are normal, and both debugging and audit need to know where each row came from."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Stamp every output row, or at least every slice, with the **logic version** (a pipeline version string or a code commit) and the **run id**. Treat any change that alters outputs as a new version, using semantic versioning or something like it. Then the scope of the backfill is \"every slice whose stamped version is older than the target version\". Keep the run's metadata, meaning its inputs' versions or snapshot ids, its logic version and its parameters, in a table of runs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** A cheap query tells you whether the backfill is complete: `SELECT count(*) WHERE logic_version <> :target`. You can tell where each restated result came from. It costs one or two columns."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** After every backfill, check that no slice in the target range carries an out-of-date version."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** First principles. It also fits lineage practice, such as OpenLineage run facets **[not cited to a specific spec section]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) A column the job sets from its build version, or dbt's `invocation_id` and a project variable. (b) The same. Unity Catalog lineage gives you lineage for tables and columns, but not a logic version for each row, so you still need the column."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.13 Schema evolution during a backfill"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** The schema of the source or the target changes while a long backfill is running, or the history contains old versions of the schema."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Old records don't have the new fields. Renaming a field breaks loaders that work by position. Widening a type is safe, but narrowing one isn't."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Freeze the target schema for the whole backfill, or make only additive changes that both the old and new code can cope with, such as new nullable columns or wider types (expand and contract, 5.1). Map each historical version of the source schema to the target schema explicitly, with a versioned mapping, rather than relying on merges by position or \"by name, as best we can\". Set incremental tools to fail on an unexpected schema change rather than quietly dropping columns (dbt's `on_schema_change: fail`) **[unverified]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** A schema change in the middle of a backfill becomes a planned step rather than a surprise. Table formats that support column ids, such as Iceberg and Delta's column mapping, make renames safe."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** The schema you snapshot at the start of the backfill should equal the one at the end, or differ only by additive changes you've recorded."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [34], and first principles."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Iceberg schema evolution by column id, and dbt's `on_schema_change`. (b) Delta schema evolution (`mergeSchema`), and column mapping mode for renames."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "5. Pattern catalogue: operational databases"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "5.1 Expand, backfill in batches, contract"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need to add or change a column on a large, busy table, and fill it in for the existing rows, without downtime."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Most forms of `ALTER TABLE` take an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock [35]. A single `UPDATE` over millions of rows is one long transaction: it holds row locks, leaves a dead tuple for every row, and holds back vacuum. And during a deploy, the old and new versions of the application run side by side."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Use parallel change, also called expand, migrate and contract [36]:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Expand.** Add the new column as nullable, or with a *non-volatile* default, which modern Postgres adds without rewriting the table [35]. Set a short `lock_timeout` on the DDL, so that a blocked `ALTER` fails quickly instead of making all the traffic queue behind it. Then deploy code that writes both the old and the new form."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Backfill in batches.** Update by ranges of primary key, a few thousand rows at a time, each in its own short transaction. Pause between batches, and throttle on replication lag (4.9). Make each batch idempotent (`WHERE new_col IS NULL`), and record your progress."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Add constraints without long locks.** Add each constraint as `NOT VALID`, then run `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`, which only takes a `SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE` lock [35]. Build indexes `CONCURRENTLY`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Move the reads over**, then **contract**: stop writing the old form, and drop it once nothing reads it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Tools can automate this. **pgroll** serves the old and new versions of the schema at the same time through views, backfills new columns in batches, uses triggers to keep the old and new columns in step during the migration, and lets you start, complete or roll back [37]. **gh-ost**, for MySQL, copies into a ghost table while following the binlog, throttles itself, and supports `--test-on-replica` [29]. Stripe's four-step dual-write migration is the same idea at the level of the application [27]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** You deploy several times instead of once, and there's a period when both forms have to be kept consistent. The triggers pgroll uses add overhead to writes."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** `SELECT count(*) WHERE new_col IS NULL` should reach zero. Spot-check that the old and new forms agree, and watch lock waits and replication lag during the run."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "36"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [27]{1}[35]{2}[37]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Postgres with pgroll, or batches you write yourself and run from Airflow, Dagster or a migration tool. (b) Lakebase is Postgres, so the same applies. Run migrations through CI against a branch first (5.2)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "5.2 Rehearse on a copy-on-write branch"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need to test an operational backfill or migration against data shaped like production, at production's size, before it touches production."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Full copies of big databases are slow and expensive. Stale copies hide exactly the edge cases that break migrations. And testing on production is risky."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "39"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Create a **copy-on-write branch** of the production database, either as it is now or as it was at some point in the past. Run the migration and backfill there, and measure how long it takes, the lock waits and the output. Then run the same script against production. Neon and Databricks Lakebase branches share storage pages with their parent and only store what changes, so creating one takes the same time whatever the database's size, and it doesn't put load on the parent [38]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Rehearsing becomes routine. A branch's compute differs from production's, so its timings are a guide, not an exact prediction. Branches contain production data, so access to them needs the same controls as production."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Rehearsal produces the same row counts and checks that the production run will be held to; production run is aborted if it diverges materially from the rehearsal."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [38]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Neon branches, or, for plain Postgres, a restored snapshot or a ZFS or LVM clone. (b) Lakebase branches. For Delta, the same pattern uses a shallow clone (4.7)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "5.3 Logical replication for initial sync"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need to fill a new Postgres database with an existing database's data, or move to a new cluster, and then keep the two in step until you cut over."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Create a publication on the source and a subscription on the target. By default, the subscription **copies the existing table data** and then streams the changes. Temporary table-synchronisation slots are created for the initial copy and dropped once it's finished [40]. Cut over when the lag is close to zero: stop writes on the source, wait for the lag to reach zero, switch the writers over, and keep the old side as a fallback."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** The schema (DDL) isn't replicated, so apply it on both sides. Sequences need resetting at cutover. The initial copy of large tables puts load on the source. And if the subscriber falls behind, the replication slots hold on to WAL, which can fill the source's disk."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Before cutover, reconcile row counts and hashes for each table at a point when writes have stopped (4.8)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [40]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Postgres's built-in logical replication. (b) Lakebase supports Postgres logical replication for syncing data in **[not verified for the current Lakebase release]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6. Pattern catalogue: legacy migration"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6.1 Strangler fig"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need to replace a legacy system that holds years of history, without a risky cutover in one go."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "28"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Build the new system alongside the old one, and move behaviour and data across a piece at a time. Keep some transitional architecture, such as adapters and event interception, so the two can run side by side, and retire the old parts as they stop being used [41]{1}. For data, migrate one domain, one region or one cohort at a time, using 6.2 to 6.4 for each piece."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** You write temporary code that you'll later delete, and you run two systems for longer. But each piece is small enough to roll back."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [41]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6.2 Initial load plus CDC cutover (or frozen source)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need to move the history across and keep up with ongoing changes until you cut over."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** There are two variants:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Live source.** Use snapshot and log stitching (4.4) or logical replication (5.3). Bulk-load the history, stream the changes, reconcile, and cut over when the lag is close to zero."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Frozen source.** If the legacy system has no usable change log, agree a **freeze point**. The legacy system becomes read-only, or you take an extract at a point in time. You load the history up to the freeze, reconcile it and get it signed off, and then the writers switch to the new system. Any write after the freeze goes only to the new system."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** A cutover from a frozen source needs an outage for writes, or a pause in the business process, but it's the easiest to verify, because there's one fixed state of the source to reconcile against. A live cutover avoids the outage, but you have to reconcile a moving target at a known position in the log."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Reconcile (4.8) at the freeze point, or at a recorded position in the log, and record the sign-off with the report."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [14]{1}[40], and first principles."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6.3 Parallel run and fallback window"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** You need confidence that the new system gives the same results as the legacy one, or deliberately different ones."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** For a set period, run both systems on the same inputs and compare their outputs (4.8). Fowler and Thoughtworks describe parallel running as the way to let the business confirm that the key numbers match before it relies on the new system [28]. After cutover, keep the legacy system available, either read-only or updated in the background, for a **fallback window**, with a tested procedure for switching back. Decide in advance what lets you stop, such as a number of consecutive cycles with no unexplained differences, and the date the fallback window ends."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Running two systems takes effort, and without an end date, a parallel run never ends."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [26]{1}[28]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6.4 Quarantine, provenance and PII minimisation"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Legacy data is messy. The backfill mustn't quietly drop bad rows, lose track of where data came from, or copy more personal data than it needs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Quarantine rows rather than dropping them.** Rows that fail validation go to a quarantine table, with the raw record, the rule they failed and the run id, so the counts reconcile as `loaded + quarantined = extracted`. Then triage the quarantined rows: fix the mapping, fix them at the source, or accept them with a recorded reason."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Record provenance on every row.** Put `source_system`, `source_key`, `source_extract_id` (or the file hash) and `loaded_by_run` on every row you load. Then you can trace any value to where it came from, and tell loaded history apart from data created in the new system."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Keep personal data to a minimum.** Decide, column by column, whether the new system needs it. Drop it, pseudonymise it (with a keyed hash and a managed secret) or generalise it before the data lands, not afterwards, because data in a landing zone or a quarantine table is still data you hold. Apply the same retention rules to quarantine and backup copies."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** There are more tables to manage, but every row is accounted for, and every value can be traced."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** For each slice, `extracted = loaded + quarantined`. No row in the target is missing its provenance columns, and an inventory of columns shows how each personal-data field is treated."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** First principles. Quarantine is the standard dead-letter or \"expectations\" approach, as in Lakeflow declarative pipeline expectations or Deequ [24]."
msgstr ""

